================================================================================
TEST T43862 -- IPv4_UnicastRouting - Secondary IP Address (Multi homing)
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-15 21:09-21:21 NZDT. Device clocks were measured against the box
          before the run: swi_d (the DUT) runs UTC, box time minus 13 h, and read
          9 s behind the box. (The two partner switches, not used by this case, read
          2 s behind and -- after a correction of a 1 m 43 s error -- 4 s ahead.)
          All times below are box time (NZDT).
Bench:    tb277, console u2 = swi_d (DUT) @115200; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T43862. NO AUTHORED STEP TEXT OR EXPECTED RESULT EXISTS for this case in
          AWPTCM or ck.db, so no step heading below quotes one; each heading names
          what was exercised instead. The limbs were derived from, and graded
          against, the X960 software TPS row 38 "Secondary IP Address (Multi
          homing)" (priority 1, supported = Yes, note column empty) and the Internet
          Protocol (IP) Addressing and Protocols Feature Overview and Configuration
          Guide, "Static IP addresses", whose whole paragraph is quoted verbatim
          below because every sentence in it is a testable claim. 5 steps plus a
          feature-OFF baseline.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon
          Oct 12 2026), AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, boot config
          flash:/default.cfg
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config after setup, before step 1; saved to startup:
          no). No partner .cfg: this case uses the DUT and the two host NICs only.

  Guide authority, verbatim:
  > "The secondary parameter allows you to add multiple IP addresses to an
  >  interface using this command. Each interface must have a primary IP address
  >  before you can add a secondary address. Your device treats secondary addresses
  >  the same as primary addresses in most respects, such as responding for ARP
  >  requests for the IP address. However, the only packets generated that have a
  >  secondary address as source address are routing updates. You can define up to
  >  32 secondary addresses on a single interface."
  Syntax: awplus(config-if)# ip address <ip-addr/prefix-length> [secondary] [label <label>]

VERDICT: PASS -- a secondary IP address on the x960-56YSQ is absent by default, and
         once configured it genuinely SERVES TRAFFIC rather than merely appearing in
         a show command: the DUT answers ARP for it, installs a connected route for
         its subnet, and forwards traffic arriving from another subnet INTO it.
         Removal takes it out of the dataplane as well as the config, both
         documented ordering rules are enforced on hardware with clear messages, and
         everything survives a real reboot.
  absent by default ................. yes        (no 172.16.10.0/24 route; ARP 0/4;
                                                  forwarding 0/5)
  ARP for the secondary ............. answered   (4/4 `ARP-REPLY 172.16.10.1 is-at
                                                  e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43`, against 0/4
                                                  with the feature off)
  connected route installed ......... yes        (`C 172.16.10.0/24 is directly
                                                  connected, vlan10`)
  forwards INTO the secondary subnet  yes        (5/5 arrived on the far NIC,
                                                  against 0/5 at baseline)
  negative control in the same leg .. dropped    (172.17.10.5: 0/5 in every leg)
  secondary used as interface identity yes       (the DUT's own ARP request reads
                                                  `tell 172.16.10.1`)
  primary required first ............ enforced   (`% Configure primary first vlan99`)
  primary cannot be removed first ... enforced   (`% Must delete secondary before
                                                  deleting primary`, config
                                                  UNCHANGED -- refused, not cascaded)
  two secondaries accepted .......... yes        (a connected route each)
  removal clears the dataplane ...... yes        (ARP 4 -> 0, forwarding 5 -> 0)
  survives a reboot ................. yes        (address, connected route, ARP and
                                                  forwarding all back after a 61 s
                                                  reload)
CAVEAT:  The guide's "up to 32 secondary addresses on a single interface" limit was
         NOT exercised: two were configured, which settled the ordering rule, and
         32 is a scale test rather than a function test. The guide's sentence "the
         only packets generated that have a secondary address as source address are
         routing updates" was NOT TESTED -- what was measured is adjacent but
         different (the DUT's own ARP requests carry `tell 172.16.10.1` as the ARP
         SENDER PROTOCOL address, which is not an IP source address). Deciding that
         sentence needs a DUT-generated IP packet aimed into the secondary subnet
         with its IP source read off the wire. The `label <label>` parameter was not
         exercised. Only an SVI was used: secondary addresses on a routed physical
         port or on an aggregator were not exercised, and neither were overlapping
         or conflicting secondaries.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   This case uses the DUT and BOTH host NICs only. Traffic crosses the DUT from
   port1.0.48 to port1.0.10 -- in at the "elsewhere" subnet, routed, out into the
   subnet the secondary address serves.

   tb277 eth4  00:0d:b9:55:77:0f           tb277 eth3  00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
