  Method: the subject is a secondary address actually SERVING TRAFFIC, not merely
  appearing in `show ip interface`. Every limb is therefore measured on the wire --
  who answers ARP, and what crosses the DUT -- with each leg carrying its own
  controls in the same capture:

    ARP legs (eth3 only), three targets per leg:
      172.16.10.1   THE SUBJECT -- the secondary address
      192.168.10.1  THE PRIMARY -- instrument control; answered whenever the sender
                    is in ITS subnet, so a zero on the subject is a measurement
      172.17.10.1   NEVER CONFIGURED -- negative control; must never be answered
    Forwarding legs (eth4 -> eth3), three destinations per leg:
      172.16.10.5   THE SUBJECT -- a host inside the secondary subnet
      192.168.10.9  THE PRIMARY SUBNET -- instrument control; must always cross
      172.17.10.5   NEVER CONFIGURED -- negative control; must never cross

STEP 0 -- baseline: no secondary address (the feature OFF)                       done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# show running-config interface vlan10
interface vlan10
 ip address 192.168.10.1/24                        <-- primary only
awplus# show ip route
C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10
C       192.168.48.0/24 is directly connected, vlan48
(no 172.16.10.0/24 anywhere)

  0a. ARP, feature OFF. --tag L1a-arp-off, sender 192.168.10.5 /
      00:11:22:33:44:01, count 4
      x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1  tell 192.168.10.5      -> 0 replies
      x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.5
      x4  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43       -> 4 replies, instrument alive
      x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.17.10.1  tell 192.168.10.5      -> 0 replies, control holds

  0b. A PLANNED DISCRIMINATOR DISCARDED AT BASELINE, because it did not
      discriminate. One candidate way to test "the secondary serves traffic" was to
      send a frame merely SOURCED in the secondary subnet (172.16.10.5 ->
      192.168.48.9, eth3 -> eth4) and count it at eth4. Run as the baseline, with
      the secondary NOT configured (--tag L1b-route-off):

        cap L1b-route-off-eth4.pcap:
          x5  ICMP t8/c0 172.16.10.5 -> 192.168.48.9  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:09]

      All five frames crossed anyway. IP forwarding here is DESTINATION based, the
      destination 192.168.48.9 is a connected subnet, and there is no uRPF, so the
      source address is not consulted at all. A leg that gives the same answer with
      the feature on and off measures nothing, so it is discarded as evidence about
      the secondary address -- and kept here as evidence of what the device does NOT
      check. The baseline-OFF leg is what caught it before it could become a
      verdict.

  0c. THE REAL DISCRIMINATOR, feature OFF: traffic from elsewhere INTO the
      secondary subnet. --tag L1c-to-secondary-off, eth4 -> watch eth3, sender
      192.168.48.9 / 00:11:22:33:44:09

      cap on eth4 (the sending side):
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 172.16.10.5    [ours]
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 192.168.10.9   [ours]
        x2  ICMP t3/c0 192.168.48.1 -> 192.168.48.9      <-- the DUT's own unreachable
      cap on eth3 (the far side):
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 192.168.10.9  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:0a]
        x1  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.9 tell 192.168.10.1 / x1 ARP-REPLY

      172.16.10.5  : 5 sent, 0 arrived on eth3 -- dropped, with the DUT reporting
                     network unreachable from its ingress address
      192.168.10.9 : 5 sent, 5 arrived -- instrument alive, the path itself works

  => STEP 0 done: with no secondary address configured, the DUT neither answers ARP
     for 172.16.10.1 (0 of 4) nor forwards anything into 172.16.10.0/24 (0 of 5),
     while the primary answers 4 of 4 and carries 5 of 5 in the same captures. Both
     zeros are measurements, not dead instruments. These are the two
     discriminators the later steps are read against; a third candidate was tried
     and discarded here because it could not tell the feature on from the feature
     off.

STEP 1 -- configure the secondary address and prove it serves traffic            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config)# interface vlan10
awplus(config-if)# ip address 172.16.10.1/24 secondary
awplus# show running-config interface vlan10
interface vlan10
 ip address 192.168.10.1/24
 ip address 172.16.10.1/24 secondary
awplus# show interface vlan10
  IPv4 address 172.16.10.1/24 broadcast 172.16.10.255 secondary
  IPv4 address 192.168.10.1/24 broadcast 192.168.10.255
awplus# show ip route
C       172.16.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10        <-- a connected route appeared
C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10

  OBSERVATION (display): `show ip interface brief` shows ONLY the primary.
awplus# show ip interface brief
vlan10                192.168.10.1/24    admin up        running
  The secondary is invisible there, while `show interface vlan10`,
  `show running-config` and `show ip route` all show it. Harmless, but
  `show ip interface brief` cannot be used to check whether a secondary exists.

  1a. ARP with the feature ON, sender in the PRIMARY subnet. --tag L2a-arp-on,
      sender 192.168.10.5:
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1  tell 192.168.10.5   -> still 0 replies
        x4  ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    -> 4 replies

  1b. THE SAME LEG WITH THE SENDER INSIDE THE SECONDARY SUBNET. --tag
      L2b-arp-on-insubnet, sender 172.16.10.5 / 00:11:22:33:44:05:
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1 tell 172.16.10.5
        x4  ARP-REPLY 172.16.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43     -> 4 replies [-> 00:11:22:33:44:05]
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 172.16.10.5    -> 0 replies
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.17.10.1  tell 172.16.10.5    -> 0 replies, control holds

      Read together, 1a and 1b are a symmetric measurement: the DUT answers ARP for
      an address on vlan10 only when the ARP SENDER's protocol address lies inside
      that address's own subnet, and this applies to the PRIMARY exactly as it does
      to the SECONDARY.
        sender 192.168.10.5 (primary subnet)   -> 192.168.10.1 answered, 172.16.10.1 silent
        sender 172.16.10.5  (secondary subnet) -> 172.16.10.1 answered, 192.168.10.1 silent
      So the guide's claim -- "treats secondary addresses the same as primary
      addresses ... such as responding for ARP requests for the IP address" --
      HOLDS exactly: both are answered under the same rule, and both are silent
      under the same rule. Taken alone, 1a would read as "the DUT does not answer
      ARP for its secondary" and would be recorded as a defect; it is not, the
      sender was simply in the wrong subnet. Both SVIs answer with the same MAC
      e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43, so only the ARP target address distinguishes them.

  1c. FORWARDING INTO THE SECONDARY SUBNET, feature ON. --tag L2c-to-secondary-on,
      eth4 -> watch eth3, sender 192.168.48.9, three destinations:

      cap on eth3 (the far side):
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 172.16.10.5   [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:05]
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 192.168.10.9  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:0a]
        x1  ARP-REQ who-has 172.16.10.5 tell 172.16.10.1   <-- the DUT ARPs AS the secondary
        x1  ARP-REPLY 172.16.10.5 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:05
      cap on eth4 (the sending side):
        x2  ICMP t3/c0 192.168.48.1 -> 192.168.48.9    <-- for the unconfigured control only

      172.16.10.5  : 5 sent, 5 arrived on eth3   (was 0 of 5 at baseline)
      192.168.10.9 : 5 sent, 5 arrived -- instrument alive
      172.17.10.5  : 5 sent, 0 arrived -- the negative control is dropped IN THE
                     SAME LEG

  => STEP 1 PASS: the secondary address is genuinely serving traffic, on three
     independent readings that all reverse the baseline. ARP for 172.16.10.1 went
     from 0 of 4 to 4 of 4 for a sender inside its subnet; a connected route for
     172.16.10.0/24 appeared; and the DUT accepted packets from another subnet,
     resolved the destination on vlan10 and delivered 5 of 5 where the identical
     leg delivered 0 at baseline -- while an unconfigured control subnet was
     dropped in the same leg, so "the DUT now forwards into 172.16.10.0/24" is not
     "the DUT now forwards anything". The DUT's own ARP request is sourced
     `tell 172.16.10.1`, the secondary address, so the device uses the secondary as
     its interface identity on that subnet exactly as a primary would. (That is an
     ARP sender protocol address, not an IP source address, so it makes no claim
     either way about the guide's routing-updates sentence, which this case did not
     test.)

STEP 2 -- the documented precondition: a primary must exist first                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Guide claim under test, verbatim: "Each interface must have a primary IP address
  before you can add a secondary address."

  Tested on a scratch SVI with no address at all, so no live segment was disturbed:

awplus(config)# vlan database
awplus(config-vlan)# vlan 99
awplus(config)# interface vlan99
awplus(config-if)# ip address 10.99.99.1/24 secondary
% Configure primary first vlan99
awplus# show running-config interface vlan99
awplus#                                    <-- empty, nothing was configured
awplus(config)# vlan database
awplus(config-vlan)# no vlan 99

  => STEP 2 PASS: the documented precondition is enforced on hardware. The command
     is refused with a message that names the interface and the reason, and nothing
     at all is written to the configuration -- the refusal is clean, not partial.

STEP 3 -- multiple secondaries, and what happens to the primary                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus(config-if)# ip address 172.16.20.1/24 secondary          <-- a SECOND secondary
awplus# show running-config interface vlan10
interface vlan10
 ip address 192.168.10.1/24
 ip address 172.16.10.1/24 secondary
 ip address 172.16.20.1/24 secondary                            <-- both accepted
awplus# show ip route
C       172.16.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10
C       172.16.20.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10            <-- a connected route each
C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, vlan10

  Then: is removing the primary refused, or does it drop the secondaries?

awplus(config-if)# no ip address 192.168.10.1/24
% Must delete secondary before deleting primary
awplus# show running-config interface vlan10
 ip address 192.168.10.1/24
 ip address 172.16.10.1/24 secondary
 ip address 172.16.20.1/24 secondary                            <-- UNCHANGED, nothing lost

  => STEP 3 PASS: more than one secondary is accepted on the same interface, each
     with its own connected route, and the ordering rule holds at the other end
     too: the primary cannot be removed while any secondary exists. It is REFUSED,
     not cascaded -- the refusal names the reason and the configuration is left
     exactly as it was, with all three addresses and both connected routes intact,
     so nothing is silently lost.

