port1.0.10  0000:e01aead47043  8392  Designated  Forwarding
port1.0.48  0000:e01aead47043  83b8  Designated  Forwarding

  5a. ARP after the reboot. --tag L6a-arp-after-reload, sender 172.16.10.5:
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.16.10.1 tell 172.16.10.5
        x4  ARP-REPLY 172.16.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43     -> 4 replies
        x4  ARP-REQ  who-has 172.17.10.1 tell 172.16.10.5     -> 0 replies, control holds

  5b. FORWARDING after the reboot. --tag L6b-route-after-reload, eth4 -> watch eth3:
      cap on eth3:
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 172.16.10.5   [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:05]
        x5  ICMP t8/c0 192.168.48.9 -> 192.168.10.9  [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:0a]
        x1  ARP-REQ who-has 172.16.10.5 tell 172.16.10.1  + its reply
        x1  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.9 tell 192.168.10.1  + its reply
      172.16.10.5  : 5 sent, 5 arrived
      192.168.10.9 : 5 sent, 5 arrived -- instrument alive
      172.17.10.5  : 5 sent, 0 arrived -- negative control still holds

      (No ICMP unreachables appeared on eth4 this time, only the DUT's ARP requests
       for 192.168.48.9: after a reboot it had to re-resolve the sender before it
       could have sent one. The unreachable is context in this case, never the
       evidence.)

  => STEP 5 PASS: after a real reboot that took 61 s to `login:`, the secondary
     address is back in the interface configuration, its connected route is back in
     the route table, it answers ARP 4 of 4 again for a sender in its subnet, and
     it carries 5 of 5 forwarded frames again -- with the negative control still at
     0 of 5 in the same leg. The secondary is not merely stored in a config file:
     it is read back and programmed into the dataplane after a restart, and the DUT
     still ARPs as 172.16.10.1 on that subnet.
